            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="RAD01" sex="2">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>Ann</forename>
                        <surname>Radcliffe</surname>
                        <addName>née Ward</addName>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1764-07-09">
                        <placeName> </placeName>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1823-02-07"/>
                    <occupation>novelist</occupation>
                    <occupation/>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Wife of <persName key="RAD02">William Radcliffe</persName>, journalist.</p>
                        <p>There is no categorical evidence that Godwin and Mrs Radcliffe met. The two diary entries that clearly refer to 
     her are 9 October 1795, 'Write to mrs Radcliffe' (the letter is not in Abinger)
     and 7 February 1823 when Godwin records her death.<lb/>
    However, we have also coded her as present when Godwin writes 'Radcliffe's', (as is the pattern with Lamb's, Reynolds's,
    Reveley's and a number of other couples).<lb/>
    This involves two entries, <lb/>
   23 April 1794: 'tea at Radcliffe's, talk of morals and fortitude'  <lb/>
    14 May 1794: 'Meet Radcliffe's'.<lb/>
The latter is particularly convincing, with Godwin characteristically mis-using the apostrophe. </p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="other"><title>DNB</title>.</bibl>
                </person>
            </listPerson>
